<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick Augustus I of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1750">1750</a>–<a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Frederick Augustus the Just, Frederick Augustus III, Elector of Saxony · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick Augustus I was elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> from 1763 and became its first king in 1806 as an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. The Treaty of Tilsit of 1807 also made him duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, a duchy he governed through ministers in Warsaw. He stayed loyal to Napoleon until he was taken prisoner after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> in 1813, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> took the duchy and much of Saxony from him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-augustus-i-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Frederick Augustus I of Saxony" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcello Bacciarelli · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederick_Augustus_I_of_Saxony_by_Marcello_Bacciarelli_(ca_1808-1809).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick Augustus was the second, but eldest surviving, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-christian-elector-of-saxony">Frederick Christian, Elector of Saxony</a>, and Maria Antonia Walpurgis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. His father died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in 1763 while he was a minor, so his mother served as regent until 1768. Two earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> had been kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, but his youth ruled him out in the Polish royal election of 1764. When the Polish Sejm ratified the constitution of 1791 it named him successor to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a>. After Stanisław's death in 1798 he declined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>, fearing entanglement in disputes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which had begun partitioning Poland in 1772.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was drawn into the war against revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and left the coalition in August 1796. Frederick Augustus took no part in founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>. In autumn 1806 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> crushed the Prussian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Auerstedt. In the peace signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a> on 11 December 1806 Saxony had to join the Confederation of the Rhine and cede parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, and in exchange became a kingdom. He was proclaimed King of Saxony on 20 December 1806.</p><p>After the Treaty of Tilsit in July 1807 he was also named Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. The duchy had an initial area of about 104,000 square kilometres and about 2,600,000 inhabitants, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>. In 1809 Polish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> troops defeated an Austrian attempt to take it, and the duchy gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and other regions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> dictated its constitution, which joined it hereditarily to the Saxon royal house.</p><p>In 1813 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> became the central arena of war. Frederick Augustus secretly pursued an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, concluded on 20 April, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> on 2 May he submitted to Napoleon's ultimatum. At the Battle of Leipzig Saxon and Polish troops fought for Napoleon, though larger Saxon formations went over to the Coalition during the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> the allies took Frederick Augustus prisoner. He was kept near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and denied a place at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>. Prussia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had agreed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kalisz-1813">Treaty of Kalisz</a> of February 1813 to compensate Prussia with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> territory, and the allies divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> on 7 January 1815. By the treaty of 21 May 1815, 57 percent of its territory and 42 percent of its population passed to Prussia. He abdicated as Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 22 May. Greeted warmly on his return in July 1815, he oversaw few political reforms in his last twelve years. He died in 1827, received the epithet The Just, and is buried in the Roman Catholic cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick Augustus I of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Seven Years’ War the Rétablissement, begun under Frederick Christian and continued under <b>Frederick Augustus III</b>, reduced debt, reformed finances and encouraged industry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>He made <b>Frederick Augustus I</b>, King of Saxony, its duke, linking it to the Kingdom of Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-warsaw">Duchy of Warsaw</a></li><li><blockquote>Elector Frederick Augustus III of Saxony changed sides after Prussia's defeat at Jena and joined the Confederation of the Rhine in December 1806, taking the title King <b>Frederick Augustus I</b>. Napoleon also made him Duke of Warsaw in 1807.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-saxony">Kingdom of Saxony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1800" aria-label="Dresden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4862 394 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick Augustus I of Saxony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1800">What was here in 1800? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
